3 Internals
***********

3.1 Bellows Engine Integration
==============================

Basalt Deep Learning never duplicates functionality already provided by
Bellows or Hyper Math:
   • CPU memory allocations are backed by 64-byte aligned
     ‘hyper_math::slab_buffer<uint8_t>’ inside
     ‘basalt::bellows_compat::Bellows_Storage’.
   • Parallel tensor kernels, batched GEMM, convolutions, and
     data-loader workers execute on ‘hyper_math::parallel_for’ and
     ‘hyper_math::thread_pool’.
   • Random number generation wraps ‘hyper_math::random_engine’ (PCG64).
   • Orthogonal weight initialization delegates to
     ‘hyper_math::scientific::linalg::qr’.
   • Tabular dataset normalization delegates to
     ‘bellows::moment_normaliser’, evaluation metrics delegate to
     ‘bellows::evaluation’, and checkpoint integrity uses
     ‘bellows::compute_fnv1a_checksum’.

3.2 Dynamic Reverse-Mode Autograd
=================================

Every differentiable operation constructs an ‘Autograd_Node’ on the
dynamic computation graph when ‘Grad_Mode::is_enabled ()’ is true and
any operand has ‘requires_grad () == true’.  Calling ‘backward ()’
performs a topological sort from the root outputs, accumulates gradients
into leaf ‘Autograd_Meta’ slots, and verifies ‘Saved_Variable’ version
counters to catch illegal in-place mutations.


